+## Deployment
+
+**Frontend → Vercel, Backend → Render.** The repo ships with `vercel.json`, `render.yaml`, and all the code changes needed for both; you only need to connect the two dashboards and set a few environment variables.
+
+### 1. Backend on Render
+
+1. In the [Render dashboard](https://dashboard.render.com/), choose **New +** → **Blueprint**, and point it at this repository. Render will read `render.yaml` and create the `cashpilot-backend` web service automatically (Python runtime, `pip install -r requirements.txt`, `uvicorn main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port $PORT`).
+2. Set the one secret it can't generate for you: **`GOOGLE_API_KEY`** (your Gemini key from [ai.google.dev](https://ai.google.dev/)). `JWT_SECRET_KEY` is auto-generated by the blueprint.
+3. Note the URL Render assigns (defaults to `https://cashpilot-backend.onrender.com`; if that name is taken, Render will suffix it — copy whatever it actually gives you).
+4. **Persistence**: `render.yaml` defaults to the Free plan, whose disk is ephemeral — the SQLite database resets on every deploy/restart. For real data retention, switch the service's plan to `starter` (or higher) and uncomment the `disk` + `DATABASE_URL` block in `render.yaml`, which mounts a persistent volume.
+
+### 2. Frontend on Vercel
+
+1. In [Vercel](https://vercel.com/new), import this repository. `vercel.json` sets `installCommand: bun install` and `buildCommand: bun run build`, and disables Vercel's zero-config framework detection (`framework: null`) so it doesn't try to treat this as a plain static Vite site.
+2. The build (`vite.config.ts` sets Nitro's `preset: "vercel"`, output pointed at `.vercel/output`) emits a [Build Output API v3](https://vercel.com/docs/build-output-api/v3) payload directly — a Node serverless function for the TanStack Start SSR shell, plus the static `public/screens/*.html` pages served as-is. No extra Vercel project settings are required.
+3. Deploy. Vercel gives you a URL like `https://cashpilot-ai.vercel.app` (or your project's actual name/domain).
+
+### 3. Wire the two together
+
+- **Backend → frontend URL**: in Render, set `CORS_ORIGINS` and `FRONTEND_URL` to your real Vercel URL(s) (comma-separated if you have more than one, e.g. a custom domain plus the `.vercel.app` one). `CORS_ORIGINS` controls which origins the browser is allowed to call the API from; `FRONTEND_URL` is used to build the password-reset link that gets logged server-side (see the note below).
+- **Frontend → backend URL**: `public/screens/api.js` picks the API URL automatically — `http://127.0.0.1:8000` when the page is served from `localhost`/`127.0.0.1`, otherwise `https://cashpilot-backend.onrender.com`. If your Render service ends up on a different hostname (name collision, custom domain, etc.), update that one line in `public/screens/api.js`, or override it per-deployment without touching code by setting `window.__CASHPILOT_API_URL__` in a `<script>` before `api.js` loads.
+
+### Known limitations to be aware of
+
+- **Password reset emails aren't sent** — no transactional email provider is wired up, so `/forgot-password` logs the reset link server-side (visible in Render's logs) instead of emailing it. Wire up SendGrid/SES/etc. before relying on this in production.
+- **Uploaded invoice files aren't cleaned up** — `/upload-invoice` writes to `backend/uploads/`, which lives on Render's local (and, on Free, ephemeral) disk.
+- **Cold starts**: Render's Free plan spins the service down after inactivity; the first request after idling can take 30–60s.
+
+---
+
